Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["1 Executive Summary", "Section",
 PageBreakAbove->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.694341537930465*^9, 3.694341550254487*^9}, {
   3.694342355079833*^9, 3.694342363410248*^9}, 3.6943428651189747`*^9, {
   3.6943430193857985`*^9, 
   3.694343019923829*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c84ad9d9-f16d-4ebd-becc-\
c5897e98c089"],

Cell["\<\
Narcissistic numbers (or Armstrong number) are numbers that can be recreated \
by summing each of their digits raised to the power of the total number of \
digits in the number itself. For example, the number 153 is a narcissistic \
number because it has three digits and satisfies the equation [1]: \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.6551323318137064`*^9, 3.655132346618133*^9}, {
  3.6558834788297663`*^9, 3.655883482105772*^9}, {3.655883581805547*^9, 
  3.655883589683561*^9}, {3.6558837346868153`*^9, 3.655883735919217*^9}, {
  3.694341574232729*^9, 3.694341596852769*^9}, {3.694342244717239*^9, 
  3.694342247462844*^9}, {3.694342333660995*^9, 3.694342343083412*^9}, {
  3.6943424202555304`*^9, 3.694342429299047*^9}, {3.6943424762647333`*^9, 
  3.694342484642213*^9}, {3.694342535512122*^9, 3.694342552269081*^9}, {
  3.6943425832578535`*^9, 3.694342597036641*^9}, {3.694342646046445*^9, 
  3.694342765915301*^9}, {3.694342880577859*^9, 3.694342892973568*^9}, {
  3.920005136849468*^9, 3.9200051373384686`*^9}, {3.9906026732401953`*^9, 
  3.9906027108125143`*^9}, {3.990602743025107*^9, 3.9906027445131073`*^9}, {
  3.991723359175848*^9, 
  3.9917233597416067`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"cb0726f4-594f-43e4-8e77-\
5d99a6d9f22b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "153 = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["1", "3"], "+", 
    SuperscriptBox["5", "3"], "+"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "03d03114-5176-4baf-b4b9-07975eed845a"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["3", "3"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "17d3e897-1cba-41cc-9f4e-2d0851c02bb0"]
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.990602750000412*^9},
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"5529d433-8b9d-4477-a466-e6b270da4de4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The objective of this project is to find as many narcissistic numbers as \
possible. A major challenge is the rapidly increasing runtime required to \
test larger numbers. A pure brute force approach checks every number \
individually by calculating the sum of its powered digits and comparing the \
result with the original number. However, the search space grows \
exponentially with the number of digits.\n\nFor example if checking a single \
number requires only 1 microsecond ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    SuperscriptBox["10", 
     RowBox[{"-", "6"}]]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "66ef6cfc-ff06-4ed5-955b-b4bbee2222eb"],
 " seconds) testing all numbers up to:\n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    SuperscriptBox["10", "6"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "2817aa09-a0d5-4a2d-a71e-9fd83d3ccf66"],
 ") numbers would already take about 1 second, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    SuperscriptBox["10", "9"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "cc151c73-c3fe-4650-bb05-99be17d176a7"],
 ") numbers would take about 17 minutes, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    SuperscriptBox["10", "12"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "63882b68-cfaf-42bb-b413-214fcfae39e5"],
 ") numbers would require around 11.5 days, and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    SuperscriptBox["10", "15"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5033b44c-3543-4191-ae3e-75ea9aa9ccd1"],
 ") numbers would take more than 31 years.\n\nBecause of this exponential \
growth, the main task of this project is to develop an optimized algorithm \
and strategies that minimize runtime as much as possible. The more efficient \
the approach is, the more narcissistic numbers can realistically be found \
within a limited amount of computation time.\n\nThis notebook tries to solve \
this problem. First, we defined the quality function for the narcissistic \
numbers. Next, we considered the brute-force approaches. In the brute-force \
approach, we check whether the function value for a given number is zero is \
first implemented sequentially and then in parallel to solve the problem. \
Among the brute-force methods, the parallelized implementation provides the \
best results. To overcome the limitations of brute-force search, the Digit \
Permutation Class approach is introduced. Unlike brute-force methods, which \
iterate through every natural number without additional constraints, this \
approach incorporates fundamental mathematical properties of narcissistic \
numbers to significantly reduce the search space. The Digit Permutation Class \
is implemented in both sequential and parallel versions. The results \
demonstrate that this approach provides a substantial performance improvement \
and represents the most efficient method for solving the given problem.\n\n\
The computation times for the approaches are recorded, and the sensitivity \
analysis then illustrates how the number of dimensions affects the runtime of \
the approaches. Based on these findings, the approaches are compared with one \
another. \n\nThe project extends beyond classical narcissistic numbers in \
base 10 by considering generalized forms, including narcissistic numbers in \
arbitrary bases, narcissistic numbers with increasing powers, constant-base \
variations, and factorion numbers. These extensions provide additional \
insights into the mathematical properties and possible generalizations of the \
problem.\n\nOverall the Digit Permutation Class approach is identified as the \
most suitable strategy for discovering the largest possible number of \
narcissistic numbers within limited computational resources due to its \
superior efficiency and scalability."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9906027815584993`*^9, 3.990602842088215*^9}, 
   3.990952910419628*^9, 
   3.9917233964054813`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"f197337f-8528-4955-958d-\
71366e0375c0"],

