Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["6 Possible Generalizations of the Problem ", "Section",
 PageBreakAbove->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.694341537930465*^9, 3.694341550254487*^9}, {
   3.694342355079833*^9, 3.694342363410248*^9}, 3.6943428651189747`*^9, {
   3.6943430193857985`*^9, 3.694343019923829*^9}, {3.6943431695433865`*^9, 
   3.694343174763685*^9}, 3.69434557823857*^9, 
   3.920005231276169*^9},ExpressionUUID->"705b9c56-25e1-487f-9053-\
b5434709e0e3"],

Cell["\<\
The concept of narcissistic numbers can be extended in several ways. The \
following generalizations explore broader mathematical ideas related to digit \
powers and number representations.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.991184593237754*^9, 3.991184599031465*^9}, 
   3.991184965499085*^9},ExpressionUUID->"ccb43663-5490-4a85-9d6d-\
71ed2905eb3f"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["6.1 Different Number Bases", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.6943454921732183`*^9, 3.694345497976429*^9}, {
  3.6943455631221437`*^9, 3.694345589018189*^9}, {3.991184620478271*^9, 
  3.9911846211034184`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4feb689e-927e-4a17-ae1e-\
d3742171e30e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In this project, narcissistic numbers are mainly investigated in base 10. \
However, the concept can be generalized to any numeral system [1].  \n\nThe \
decimal number ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["62", "10"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7f1f851c-bac3-4245-9522-d55f0c15a615"],
 ", represented as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["332", "4"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "6428a73c-c6d7-4b90-843b-e4159c527f85"],
 "\:200b in base 4, is a narcissistic number in base 4.\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]\n\nVerification: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"3", "*", 
    SuperscriptBox["4", "4"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "4f5ec091-45f5-4e79-8c95-6c420354de80"],
 " + ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"3", "*", 
    SuperscriptBox["4", "1"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "58e5f499-2271-4264-b976-ff206de06e2a"],
 " ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"+", " ", "2"}], "*", 
    SuperscriptBox["4", "0"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "1e8b3d77-5b9a-427c-aa2e-6f9821df62c2"],
 " = 48 + 12 + 2 = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["62", "10"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5b9d53a8-88f2-477f-a9c1-d8728207d7ab"],
 "\n\nSince ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["332", "4"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "e879757b-9c5a-4cdc-8c8c-6990abd7274a"],
 " consists of three digits, each digit is raised to the third power: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["3", "3"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5bf43b74-9d3b-42d4-9373-f274c2a4a4cd"],
 " + ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["3", "3"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3c66c4b5-ae66-4348-bd1e-acb07007ea70"],
 " + ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"2", "^", "3"}], " ", "=", " ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"27", " ", "+", " ", "27", " ", "+", " ", "8"}], " ", "=", " ", 
     SubscriptBox["62", "10"]}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "4f82563a-83bf-4434-a9ec-b21ee087b280"],
 "\n\nTherefore, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["62", "10"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "247f96ce-b13e-4775-b214-a8538074a5a0"],
 " = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["332", "4"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "bfa393de-2921-45a3-b2f6-fa443b0d5be9"],
 " = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["3", "3"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "2e5b2d38-91de-4c32-a0ac-44ae6e3acd1d"],
 " + ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["3", "3"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "38bacacf-39a0-44dc-89b2-34b1464c63f5"],
 " + ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["2", "3"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "a551e75a-043d-44d5-a0ae-edb514a39584"],
 " which proves that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["62", "10"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3cc625d8-14e2-46b2-ba2e-b140fde05ebf"],
 " is a narcissistic number in base 4."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->CompressedData["
1:eJxTTMoPSmViYGAQBWIQ/Yizauoc69eO+48HTwfRRb23orhtXzvWJDlHg2ju
4zvTQfTu+1szQHTKr6tVINoq6y+Ybik6viO17bXjI8/Zu0H0/M/TeNKA9Lzz
78D0fbWt4iA6YPUjCRDtp9uhBKJPT1ypDKI3VXRogmim3ilaYPnkDwZg9XM4
jEF0mMMLMxCtpbPQAkRX1Ry0A9GqXnt9QfSlWqEYEF2hPj8WRN/Qk2a6aPLW
cc8VLTB9Z1vJvCl73jre5Ji6EEQzNHeeAdGPBITPgei7m9lfguiuye7vQfSZ
mCtfQPQSmQlMU4G0m+hKYRDtsP+ZFojemCtZB6KLmcPaQHSFY9Kq2XvfOs76
XgWmAbc4rIY=
  "],ExpressionUUID->"90483382-5eb0-d448-a807-fd35b3697047"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["6.2 Generalizations and Variants of Narcissistic Numbers", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.6943454921732183`*^9, 3.6943455089276485`*^9}, {
   3.694345564760146*^9, 3.694345590640591*^9}, {3.991185827214696*^9, 
   3.991185827696772*^9}, 
   3.991186394877441*^9},ExpressionUUID->"0d2b20f8-5a84-48aa-a501-\
ba40a886834b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In addition to the classical definition, several related types of \
narcissistic numbers can be found [1].\n\nNarcissistic numbers with \
increasing exponents: 2427 = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["2", "1"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "9a07752d-c096-4e2b-8553-78cb223778bb"],
 " + ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["4", 
    RowBox[{"2", " "}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "180b19c9-4ec0-40e8-a99d-5e7d6d78f0c5"],
 "+ ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["2", "3"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "6f7d8606-3421-402f-aa11-a89a3927198e"],
 " + ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["7", "4"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "d93adc01-a1ec-4252-9f76-af4e62a190df"],
 " = 2 + 16 + 8 + 2401 = 2427\n\nNarcissistic numbers with constant base: \
4624 = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["4", 
    RowBox[{"4", " "}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "a128ec68-b8cc-4829-8ede-d6cb9aeaddb4"],
 "+ ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["4", "6"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "e73a96f3-a11e-433b-8eb6-21c719807843"],
 " + ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["4", "2"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "1de8fa59-f810-4084-b385-bbef8b90b155"],
 "+ ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["4", "4"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "8dcdef1b-b1b4-4fd5-a88e-a22fa510fbe6"],
 " = 256 + 4096 + 16 + 256 = 4624\n\nFactorion Numbers: 145 = 1! + 4! + 5! = \
1 + 24 + 120 = 145"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->CompressedData["
1:eJxTTMoPSmViYGAQBWIQ/Yizauoc69eO+48HTwfRRb23orhtXzvWJDlHg2ju
4zvTQfTu+1szQHTKr6tVINoq6y+Ybik6viO17bXjI8/Zu0H0/M/TeNKA9Lzz
78D0fbWt4iA6YPUjCRDtp9uhBKJPT1ypDKI3VXRogmim3ilaYPnkDwZg9XM4
jEF0mMMLMxCtpbPQAkRX1Ry0A9GqXnt9QfSlWqEYEF2hPj8WRN/Qk2a6aPLW
cc8VLTAtsTfVdOqet47/LFxcQfSc29t8QfSJ9xf8QXTVH5loEO2TFxoPoufK
h6WA6CMsCWA69m1ZFojukZbMBdFvTs8rApv3bCWYfsu/duXsvW8dJxbcBdMA
Sw6tPA==
  "],ExpressionUUID->"9364fb2f-91f2-e644-87b9-8cdfc42e96db"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["7 Consequenses and Recommendations", "Section",
 PageBreakAbove->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.694341537930465*^9, 3.694341550254487*^9}, {
   3.694342355079833*^9, 3.694342363410248*^9}, 3.6943428651189747`*^9, {
   3.6943430193857985`*^9, 3.694343019923829*^9}, {3.6943431695433865`*^9, 
   3.694343174763685*^9}, 3.694345609313824*^9, 
   3.920005243579571*^9},ExpressionUUID->"e9bfba02-bbb8-47a6-8db6-\
d4f16dbe0f14"],

Cell["\<\
Based on the results of the previous sections, the brute force approach \
should mainly be used as a baseline for validation and comparison. It is \
simple to understand and easy to implement, but it does not scale well \
because every number in the selected range has to be checked individually.

The digit permutation class approach is recommended as the main algorithmic \
strategy. By evaluating digit compositions instead of individual numbers, it \
reduces redundant calculations and makes the search process significantly \
less sensitive to increasing digit length.

Parallelization should be applied depending on the size of the search \
problem. For smaller dimensions, the non-parallel digit permutation class \
approach can be faster because it avoids parallelization overhead. For larger \
dimensions, the parallelized version becomes more useful, because the \
workload is large enough to benefit from multiple kernels.

Therefore, the recommended final strategy is to use the digit permutation \
class approach and choose between the non-parallel and parallelized \
implementation depending on the tested digit dimension and available hardware.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.6551323318137064`*^9, 3.655132346618133*^9}, {
   3.6558834788297663`*^9, 3.655883482105772*^9}, {3.655883581805547*^9, 
   3.655883589683561*^9}, {3.6558837346868153`*^9, 3.655883735919217*^9}, {
   3.694341574232729*^9, 3.694341596852769*^9}, {3.694342244717239*^9, 
   3.694342247462844*^9}, {3.694342333660995*^9, 3.694342343083412*^9}, {
   3.6943424202555304`*^9, 3.694342429299047*^9}, {3.6943424762647333`*^9, 
   3.694342484642213*^9}, {3.694342535512122*^9, 3.694342552269081*^9}, {
   3.6943425832578535`*^9, 3.694342597036641*^9}, {3.694342646046445*^9, 
   3.694342765915301*^9}, {3.694342880577859*^9, 3.694342892973568*^9}, {
   3.920005136849468*^9, 3.9200051373384686`*^9}, {3.991559971525507*^9, 
   3.991559976582161*^9}, {3.991560037214815*^9, 3.991560053488508*^9}, 
   3.9915617928188496`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"2a1205f6-5c69-4b47-bba4-\
e02703d86a7d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["8 Summary of results", "Section",
 PageBreakAbove->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.694341537930465*^9, 3.694341550254487*^9}, {
   3.694342355079833*^9, 3.694342363410248*^9}, 3.6943428651189747`*^9, {
   3.6943430193857985`*^9, 3.694343019923829*^9}, {3.6943431695433865`*^9, 
   3.694343174763685*^9}, 3.6943456199998417`*^9, 
   3.92000525231802*^9},ExpressionUUID->"8ec61a4a-53fe-4595-983f-\
27013f567dad"],

Cell["\<\
This notebook investigated different algorithmic approaches for identifying \
narcissistic numbers in base 10. The problem was first transformed into an \
optimization perspective by defining a quality function. For narcissistic \
numbers, this function returns zero, which makes them the global minima of \
the function.

The first implemented method was the brute force approach. It checks every \
number in the selected range and is therefore simple and transparent. \
However, the runtime measurements show that this approach becomes inefficient \
as the digit length increases, because the number of candidates grows rapidly.

The parallelized brute force approach improves the runtime by distributing \
the evaluations across multiple Mathematica kernels. This reduces computation \
time for larger ranges, but it does not change the underlying structure of \
the algorithm. Every number still has to be checked individually.

The most important improvement was achieved by the digit permutation class \
approach. Instead of evaluating every number separately, this method \
evaluates digit compositions. Since numbers with the same digit composition \
have the same digit power sum, redundant calculations can be avoided. This \
significantly reduces the effective search space and improves scalability.

The sensitivity analysis confirmed that the runtime depends strongly on the \
digit dimension and on the chosen algorithmic approach. It also showed that \
parallelization is useful only when the workload is large enough to \
compensate for its overhead. For smaller dimensions, the non-parallel digit \
permutation class approach can be faster, while for larger dimensions the \
parallelized version becomes more advantageous.

Overall, the project shows that the most effective strategy is not brute \
force alone, but a combination of mathematical structure and selective \
parallelization. The digit permutation class approach provides the central \
algorithmic improvement, while parallelization can further accelerate the \
computation for sufficiently large search spaces.
\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.6551323318137064`*^9, 3.655132346618133*^9}, {
  3.6558834788297663`*^9, 3.655883482105772*^9}, {3.655883581805547*^9, 
